From abe439d3c6a40a33da67f68bafccf1a8c1200707 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 16:59:44 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] egl/haiku: Zink when there is a Vulkan GPU, else the
 software rasteriser

The Haiku EGL driver created a Zink screen unconditionally, which made
sense while Zink came from a Quasar-only build. Renga now packages a
system Mesa (RengaPorts mesa 26.2.2: llvmpipe, lavapipe, glvnd, EGL). The
clean arrangement is one system Mesa whose EGL uses Zink on whatever
Vulkan GPU driver is installed (NVK through Quasar on NVIDIA) and
llvmpipe everywhere else, rather than two libEGL_mesa vendors, one per
package.

haiku_create_screen():
- tries zink_create_screen() first. Zink refuses CPU Vulkan devices
  (lavapipe) unless asked for one, so a machine without a GPU driver
  falls through;
- otherwise uses sw_screen_create() (llvmpipe or softpipe, whichever is
  built);
- honours GALLIUM_DRIVER=llvmpipe/softpipe and LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE
  for the software path, as on other platforms;
- logs at info level when it falls back.

Each branch is compiled only when its driver is built: Quasar's Zink-only
build still fails eglInitialize without a GPU, as before.

hgl.c: the GALLIUM_ZINK block that aliases the back buffer to the front
drawable now also requires the screen to have resource_create_drawable,
which only Zink has. A software screen in the same build keeps a display
target per buffer, as upstream hgl does.

Checked: tools/crosscheck/mesa.sh for both files, and for egl_haiku.cpp
also with GALLIUM_ZINK and GALLIUM_LLVMPIPE both defined. Not built with
GCC or run.

